  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 355 AM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nTransient ridging will try to build in over the area Sunday into \nMonday, but we won't see much of a warm-up if any. 700-mb \ntemperatures peak in the morning and then drop down again through \nthe day Monday as the next messy Pacific trough approaches the area. \nIn addition, the surface high pressure system will remain entrenched \nover the plains while increasing cloud cover prevents much warming. \nThe approaching system will be another disorganized, split trough \nwithout much moisture or organized lift. Most ensemble members have \nsome light snow developing in the mountains Monday PM and continuing \ninto Tuesday AM, but even orographic ascent looks fairly weak, \ntempering QPF expectations. The mountains may manage to pick up a \nfew inches on the high end. Tuesday looks like another chilly day, \nbut with more sunshine as the trough pushes off to the east. \nWesterly winds will also return, but high wind probabilities are \nonly around 10%. These creep up slightly Tuesday night to around 30 \nto 40% as pressure/height gradients tighten. \n\nMore organized ridging is expected to return to the area Wednesday, \ninitiating another warming trend. Highs should be near to a few \ndegrees above seasonal averages, generally mid 40s to lower 50s, and \nthen remain near that mark for the rest of the work-week (possibly \nadding a few degrees Thursday or Friday). There is quite a bit of \nmodel spread in the late week period around Thanksgiving Day. The \noverall evolution of the synoptic pattern will likely feature \nanother disorganized, moisture-starved trough meandering through on \nThursday, followed by riding re-establishing itself Friday. \nEnsembles have fairly good agreement within a given system, but are \nextremely divergent between suites (e.g. GEFS vs ECMWF ensemble). \nNearly all GEFS members push the Thursday low so far to the south \nthat our area remains entirely dry. Then, the resultant ridge Friday \namplifies further north, centered over the Pacific Northwest, \nforcing the storm track and jet stream well to our north into \nCanada. This would lead to little to no precipitation anywhere in \nthe area, significantly warmer temperatures, and lighter winds. \nMeanwhile, most ECMWF ensemble members are clustered around a \nsolution that keeps the first upper level low closer to our area, \nbringing a chance for some light snow showers Wednesday night. Then, \nthe developing ridge is strongest over the southwest CONUS instead \nof northwest, allowing the core of the Pacific jet to stream inland \nmuch closer to our area. This scenario would keep temperatures \ncloser to seasonal averages, increase the potential for mountain \nsnowfall Thursday and Friday, and also dramatically increase the \npotential for high winds across southeast Wyoming. The GEFS has \ngenerally been (anecdotally) performing better than the ECMWF \nensemble at this lead time in recent weeks, but that doesn't \nnecessarily mean it will continue to perform better. The official \nforecast represents a blend of these two somewhat equally likely \nscenarios.",
